<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martin Buber</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mordechai Buber</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martin Buber was an Austrian-Israeli philosopher, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, best known for his philosophy of dialogue, which distinguished the I–Thou relationship from the I–It relationship. Raised by his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Buber in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lemberg</a>, he broke with Jewish custom to study philosophy and joined the Zionist movement in 1898. In 1923 he wrote Ich und Du (I and Thou), and in 1925 he began translating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> into German. He resigned his professorship at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in protest when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> came to power in 1933, settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1938 and taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> University. He died at his home in Jerusalem in 1965.</p></div>
